{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-47361" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/assets/fa3045b53d5a91ae7adbf450243338b988239749e4ae7340938943cce4c239fb.png)\n\nAn oil tank is leaking $q(t)$ liters of oil after $t$ hours.\n The graph of $q(t)$ is shown above.\n\nApproximate the rate at which the oil is leaking from the tank when $t=2$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/state.json" "file_sha256" "8edc829ec615614185714eb43b13b0593963b9000cb298aeb3d9691faca5b246"} {"question" "q-47361" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{q(t+h)-q(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=2$ and step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{q(2+1)-q(2-1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(3)-q(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-2}{2} \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of leaking at the instant of time $t=2$ is $0.5\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/state.json" "file_sha256" "8edc829ec615614185714eb43b13b0593963b9000cb298aeb3d9691faca5b246"} {"question" "q-47361" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "599534d9a969b529fa4647d56016b29af1f47246715998b50df9906b48f551f5"} {"question" "q-47361" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/state.json" "file_sha256" "8edc829ec615614185714eb43b13b0593963b9000cb298aeb3d9691faca5b246"} {"question" "q-47361" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.5\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}"] ["math" "1.5\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}"] ["math" "1\\,\\text{liter}/\\text{hour}"] ["math" "2.5\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}"] ["math" "2\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/state.json" "file_sha256" "8edc829ec615614185714eb43b13b0593963b9000cb298aeb3d9691faca5b246"} {"question" "q-47361" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.5\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/state.json" "file_sha256" "8edc829ec615614185714eb43b13b0593963b9000cb298aeb3d9691faca5b246"} {"question" "q-51405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/assets/1e11260301be8c0bb19a9fd47ffbbf71cc5fa4d94dbeae57af934b76fb17f0e1.png)\n\nA particle has a speed of $v(t)$ meters per second after $t$ seconds. The graph of $v(t)$ is shown above. Approximate the rate of change of speed when $t=3$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/state.json" "file_sha256" "8edc829ec615614185714eb43b13b0593963b9000cb298aeb3d9691faca5b246"} {"question" "q-51405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{v}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{v(t+h)-g(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=2$ and step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{v(3+1)-v(3-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{v(4)-v(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of speed at the instant of time $t=2$ is approximately $1\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/state.json" "file_sha256" "8edc829ec615614185714eb43b13b0593963b9000cb298aeb3d9691faca5b246"} {"question" "q-51405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "599534d9a969b529fa4647d56016b29af1f47246715998b50df9906b48f551f5"} {"question" "q-51405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/state.json" "file_sha256" "8edc829ec615614185714eb43b13b0593963b9000cb298aeb3d9691faca5b246"} {"question" "q-51405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.5\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}"] ["math" "0.75\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}"] ["math" "1\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}"] ["math" "2\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}"] ["math" "3\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/state.json" "file_sha256" "8edc829ec615614185714eb43b13b0593963b9000cb298aeb3d9691faca5b246"} {"question" "q-51405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/state.json" "file_sha256" "8edc829ec615614185714eb43b13b0593963b9000cb298aeb3d9691faca5b246"} {"question" "q-314898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/assets/d27b0d70171683240fd44c8bbbcc8cb46ce0f0f216533a8061d31a991cce4e14.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=2$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=1$ and $x=3.$*\n\n${f}^{′}(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/state.json" "file_sha256" "8edc829ec615614185714eb43b13b0593963b9000cb298aeb3d9691faca5b246"} {"question" "q-314898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=2$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{f(2+1)-f(2-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3)-f(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)}{2} \\\\  & =-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/state.json" "file_sha256" "8edc829ec615614185714eb43b13b0593963b9000cb298aeb3d9691faca5b246"} {"question" "q-314898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "599534d9a969b529fa4647d56016b29af1f47246715998b50df9906b48f551f5"} {"question" "q-314898" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/state.json" "file_sha256" "8edc829ec615614185714eb43b13b0593963b9000cb298aeb3d9691faca5b246"} {"question" "q-314898" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-0.5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/state.json" "file_sha256" "8edc829ec615614185714eb43b13b0593963b9000cb298aeb3d9691faca5b246"} {"question" "q-344023" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/assets/5663e7c59a9f42b31ebadd9abb54e36c604526c00334f840014a674c5b6dcdb8.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=6$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=3.$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=3$ and $x=9.$*\n\n${f}^{′}(6)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/state.json" "file_sha256" "8edc829ec615614185714eb43b13b0593963b9000cb298aeb3d9691faca5b246"} {"question" "q-344023" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=6$ with step size $h=3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(6) & ≈\\frac{f(6+3)-f(6-3)}{2(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(9)-f(3)}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-6}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)}{6} \\\\  & =-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/state.json" "file_sha256" "8edc829ec615614185714eb43b13b0593963b9000cb298aeb3d9691faca5b246"} {"question" "q-344023" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "599534d9a969b529fa4647d56016b29af1f47246715998b50df9906b48f551f5"} {"question" "q-344023" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.typ